1
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,506
Welcome to the British Museum in London,

2
00:00:06,526 --> 00:00:09,061
one of the greatest museums in the world.

3
00:00:09,461 --> 00:00:10,908
It was founded in 1753,

4
00:00:10,928 --> 00:00:14,097
when the doctor and collector Sir Hans Sloane left

5
00:00:14,117 --> 00:00:17,902
his collection of more than 71,000 objects to the nation.

6
00:00:18,302 --> 00:00:21,704
It opened its doors in 1759, as the first free,

7
00:00:21,724 --> 00:00:24,569
national, public museum in the world.

8
00:00:24,969 --> 00:00:27,847
The grand building in front of us was designed by

9
00:00:27,867 --> 00:00:31,100
Sir Robert Smirke to look like an ancient Greek temple,

10
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:33,163
with 44 columns along its front.

11
00:00:33,563 --> 00:00:36,872
Today, the museum cares for around eight million objects,

12
00:00:36,892 --> 00:00:39,612
covering two million years of human history.

13
00:00:39,762 --> 00:00:41,360
Let's head inside.

14
00:00:44,710 --> 00:00:47,348
This is the Queen Elizabeth II Great Court,

15
00:00:47,368 --> 00:00:49,001
the heart of the museum.

16
00:00:49,401 --> 00:00:51,561
For nearly 150 years, this courtyard

17
00:00:51,581 --> 00:00:54,456
was crammed with bookshelves for the library.

18
00:00:54,606 --> 00:00:56,678
When the British Library moved out,

19
00:00:56,698 --> 00:00:59,189
architects Foster and Partners transformed

20
00:00:59,209 --> 00:01:01,161
it with a spectacular glass roof,

21
00:01:01,181 --> 00:01:03,124
which opened in the year 2000.

22
00:01:03,524 --> 00:01:06,612
The roof is made of 3,312 panes of glass,

23
00:01:06,632 --> 00:01:10,268
and no two of them are exactly the same shape.

24
00:01:10,668 --> 00:01:14,943
In the middle stands the round Reading Room, opened in 1857.

25
00:01:15,093 --> 00:01:17,824
Its readers included Karl Marx, Bram Stoker,

26
00:01:17,844 --> 00:01:20,620
the author of Dracula, and Mahatma Gandhi.

27
00:01:21,020 --> 00:01:23,956
Our first gallery is just through the doors to the west.

28
00:01:27,306 --> 00:01:28,695
In Room 4, we find the

29
00:01:28,715 --> 00:01:31,875
museum's most famous object: the Rosetta Stone.

30
00:01:32,275 --> 00:01:35,301
It is a broken slab of dark granodiorite,

31
00:01:35,321 --> 00:01:38,570
carved in Egypt in 196 BC with a decree from

32
00:01:38,590 --> 00:01:42,678
a council of priests about the young king, Ptolemy V.

33
00:01:43,078 --> 00:01:44,623
What makes it special is that

34
00:01:44,643 --> 00:01:46,781
the same message is written three times,

35
00:01:46,801 --> 00:01:48,408
in three different scripts.

36
00:01:48,808 --> 00:01:50,386
At the top are hieroglyphs,

37
00:01:50,406 --> 00:01:52,864
used for important and religious texts.

38
00:01:53,014 --> 00:01:56,357
In the middle is Demotic, the everyday script of Egypt.

39
00:01:56,507 --> 00:01:58,757
And at the bottom is ancient Greek.

40
00:01:59,157 --> 00:02:01,913
French soldiers found the stone in 1799,

41
00:02:01,933 --> 00:02:05,970
near the town of Rashid, which Europeans called Rosetta.

42
00:02:06,120 --> 00:02:08,838
It came to the British Museum in 1802.

43
00:02:09,238 --> 00:02:12,535
Scholars could already read the Greek, so they used it as a key.

44
00:02:12,685 --> 00:02:15,763
In 1822, Jean-François Champollion announced that

45
00:02:15,783 --> 00:02:18,228
he had cracked the code of hieroglyphs,

46
00:02:18,248 --> 00:02:20,693
starting with royal names like Ptolemy,

47
00:02:20,713 --> 00:02:23,707
written inside oval shapes called cartouches.

48
00:02:27,057 --> 00:02:31,308
Next, we step into ancient Assyria, in what is now northern Iraq.

49
00:02:31,708 --> 00:02:34,364
These giant guardians are called lamassu.

50
00:02:34,514 --> 00:02:35,864
Each has the head of a man,

51
00:02:35,884 --> 00:02:38,267
the body of a bull, and the wings of a bird.

52
00:02:38,667 --> 00:02:40,831
They once stood at the gateways

53
00:02:40,851 --> 00:02:43,649
of King Sargon II's palace at Khorsabad,

54
00:02:43,669 --> 00:02:46,778
about 2,700 years ago, to scare away evil.

55
00:02:47,178 --> 00:02:48,808
Now count their legs.

56
00:02:48,958 --> 00:02:51,777
From the front, a lamassu stands firmly still.

57
00:02:51,927 --> 00:02:53,998
From the side, it strides forward.

58
00:02:54,148 --> 00:02:57,584
To make both views work, the sculptors gave it five legs!

59
00:02:57,984 --> 00:03:00,118
Close by, in Room 10a, you can see

60
00:03:00,138 --> 00:03:03,518
the famous lion hunt carvings of King Ashurbanipal.

61
00:03:06,868 --> 00:03:09,627
Room 18 holds sculptures from the Parthenon,

62
00:03:09,647 --> 00:03:12,091
the great temple of the goddess Athena,

63
00:03:12,111 --> 00:03:13,966
on the Acropolis in Athens.

64
00:03:14,366 --> 00:03:17,803
They were carved between 447 and 432 BC,

65
00:03:17,823 --> 00:03:21,775
under the direction of the sculptor Phidias.

66
00:03:22,175 --> 00:03:25,923
The long marble frieze shows a grand procession of horse riders,

67
00:03:25,943 --> 00:03:29,921
chariots, musicians and animals, making its way to honour Athena.

68
00:03:30,321 --> 00:03:32,883
Between 1801 and 1812, agents of Lord

69
00:03:32,903 --> 00:03:36,582
Elgin removed about half of the surviving sculptures,

70
00:03:36,602 --> 00:03:40,241
and in 1816 Parliament bought them for the museum.

71
00:03:40,641 --> 00:03:43,615
Greece has long asked for them to be returned to Athens,

72
00:03:43,635 --> 00:03:46,726
and the debate about where they belong continues today.

73
00:03:50,076 --> 00:03:53,826
In Room 24 stands Hoa Hakananai'a, a statue, or moai,

74
00:03:53,846 --> 00:03:58,620
from Rapa Nui, also known as Easter Island, in the Pacific Ocean.

75
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:02,966
He was carved from volcanic basalt around 800 to 1,000 years ago,

76
00:04:02,986 --> 00:04:06,065
and he stands almost two and a half metres tall.

77
00:04:06,465 --> 00:04:10,163
His name is often translated as 'lost or stolen friend'.

78
00:04:10,563 --> 00:04:11,960
Look at his back.

79
00:04:12,110 --> 00:04:13,974
It was later carved with birdmen,

80
00:04:13,994 --> 00:04:15,630
linked to a contest held each

81
00:04:15,650 --> 00:04:18,142
year at the ceremonial village of Orongo.

82
00:04:18,542 --> 00:04:21,010
In 1868, the crew of a British ship,

83
00:04:21,030 --> 00:04:23,737
HMS Topaze, took him from the island.

84
00:04:23,887 --> 00:04:27,362
Today, the Rapa Nui people have asked for him to come home.

85
00:04:30,712 --> 00:04:32,976
Let's head upstairs to Room 56,

86
00:04:32,996 --> 00:04:36,610
and a board game that's around 4,500 years old.

87
00:04:37,010 --> 00:04:39,125
This is the Royal Game of Ur.

88
00:04:39,275 --> 00:04:42,701
It was found in a royal tomb in the ancient city of Ur,

89
00:04:42,721 --> 00:04:44,205
in southern Iraq, by the

90
00:04:44,225 --> 00:04:47,069
archaeologist Leonard Woolley in the 1920s.

91
00:04:47,469 --> 00:04:49,492
The board is decorated with shell,

92
00:04:49,512 --> 00:04:51,825
red limestone and blue lapis lazuli.

93
00:04:51,975 --> 00:04:55,024
Two players raced their pieces along its twenty squares.

94
00:04:55,424 --> 00:04:57,881
For a long time, nobody knew the rules.

95
00:04:58,031 --> 00:05:01,824
Then curator Irving Finkel studied a clay tablet,

96
00:05:01,844 --> 00:05:06,507
written much later in 177 BC, which explained how to play.

97
00:05:06,907 --> 00:05:09,524
Land on a rosette, and you get another turn.

98
00:05:09,674 --> 00:05:11,505
People still play it today!

99
00:05:14,855 --> 00:05:17,048
Rooms 62 and 63 are home to some

100
00:05:17,068 --> 00:05:21,436
of the museum's most popular residents: the Egyptian mummies.

101
00:05:21,836 --> 00:05:24,996
Ancient Egyptians believed the body had to be preserved,

102
00:05:25,016 --> 00:05:27,722
so the spirit could live on in the afterlife.

103
00:05:28,122 --> 00:05:30,326
Embalmers removed the internal organs,

104
00:05:30,346 --> 00:05:32,726
dried the body with a salt called natron,

105
00:05:32,746 --> 00:05:35,589
and wrapped it in hundreds of metres of linen.

106
00:05:35,739 --> 00:05:38,263
The whole process could take seventy days.

107
00:05:38,663 --> 00:05:41,825
The body was then placed inside brightly painted coffins,

108
00:05:41,845 --> 00:05:45,400
covered with prayers and spells to protect it on its journey.

109
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:47,457
It wasn't just people.

110
00:05:47,607 --> 00:05:49,603
Egyptians also mummified cats,

111
00:05:49,623 --> 00:05:53,268
birds and even crocodiles, as offerings to the gods.

112
00:05:56,618 --> 00:06:00,218
Now we travel to England, around 1,400 years ago.

113
00:06:00,368 --> 00:06:03,097
Room 41 holds the treasures of Sutton Hoo.

114
00:06:03,497 --> 00:06:06,556
In 1939, on Edith Pretty's land in Suffolk,

115
00:06:06,576 --> 00:06:10,852
Basil Brown began to uncover the outline of a 27-metre ship,

116
00:06:10,872 --> 00:06:13,743
buried beneath a great mound of earth.

117
00:06:14,143 --> 00:06:15,462
The wood had rotted away,

118
00:06:15,482 --> 00:06:19,007
but rows of iron rivets showed exactly where the ship had been.

119
00:06:19,157 --> 00:06:22,166
In the middle was a burial chamber, filled with treasure.

120
00:06:22,566 --> 00:06:25,981
It was probably the grave of an Anglo-Saxon king,

121
00:06:26,001 --> 00:06:30,496
perhaps Rædwald, ruler of East Anglia, who died around AD 624.

122
00:06:30,896 --> 00:06:33,431
The star object is this iron helmet.

123
00:06:33,581 --> 00:06:35,724
Look closely at the face: the eyebrows,

124
00:06:35,744 --> 00:06:39,054
nose and moustache form a dragon with outstretched wings.

125
00:06:39,454 --> 00:06:42,842
Edith Pretty generously gave the whole treasure to the nation.

126
00:06:46,192 --> 00:06:49,152
Next door, in Room 40, meet the Lewis Chessmen.

127
00:06:49,552 --> 00:06:52,653
They were found in 1831 on the Isle of Lewis,

128
00:06:52,673 --> 00:06:55,011
in the Outer Hebrides of Scotland,

129
00:06:55,031 --> 00:06:59,065
and were probably carved in Norway around 850 years ago.

130
00:06:59,465 --> 00:07:01,618
Most are made of walrus ivory.

131
00:07:01,768 --> 00:07:03,994
There are kings, queens, bishops, knights,

132
00:07:04,014 --> 00:07:06,088
and warders, which act as the rooks.

133
00:07:06,488 --> 00:07:08,647
Look out for the worried-looking queens,

134
00:07:08,667 --> 00:07:10,608
resting their cheeks on their hands,

135
00:07:10,628 --> 00:07:14,374
and the fierce warriors biting their shields, known as berserkers.

136
00:07:14,774 --> 00:07:16,605
82 pieces are here in London,

137
00:07:16,625 --> 00:07:20,542
and 11 are in the National Museum of Scotland in Edinburgh.

138
00:07:20,692 --> 00:07:22,583
They even inspired the wizard's chess

139
00:07:22,603 --> 00:07:24,355
in the first Harry Potter film!

140
00:07:27,705 --> 00:07:29,086
That's the end of our tour,

141
00:07:29,106 --> 00:07:31,644
but we've only seen a tiny part of the museum.

142
00:07:32,044 --> 00:07:33,654
Only about one per cent of the

143
00:07:33,674 --> 00:07:35,881
collection is on display at any one time,

144
00:07:35,901 --> 00:07:38,441
so there's always something new to discover.

145
00:07:38,841 --> 00:07:40,959
The museum also raises big questions:

146
00:07:40,979 --> 00:07:42,634
whose stories are being told,

147
00:07:42,654 --> 00:07:45,000
and where should these objects belong?

148
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:47,873
Entry to the permanent collection is free.

149
00:07:48,023 --> 00:07:50,834
Thanks for joining the tour, and happy exploring!

